---
/**
 * K Culture Wire — **형제가 옆에 있으면.** (`/siblings`)
 *
 * ── ⛔ 이 지면이 지키는 것 ────────────────────────────────────
 * ⛔ **작은 것을 크게 안 말한다.** 차이는 열 칸 중 **반 칸**이다. 그것이 답이다.
 * ⛔ **버린 것을 밝힌다.** 짝이 없어 버린 주가 견준 짝보다 훨씬 많다.
 * ⛔ **처음 설계가 틀렸던 것을 적는다** — 나이를 딱 맞추니 표본이 대표성을 잃었다.
 * ⛔ 왜 그런지는 이 자료에 **없다.** 자리를 뺏은 것인지 같이 뜬 것인지 못 가른다.
 * ⛔ 광고 자리를 만들지 않았다. Riot Production(App 866800) 승인 전이다.
 * ⚠ 수를 손으로 적지 않는다. 전부 `wikitip-siblings.json` 에서 읽는다.
 */
import WikiTip from '../../layouts/WikiTip.astro';
import KcwRelatedArticles from '../../components/KcwRelatedArticles.astro';
import data from '../../data/wikitip-siblings.json';

const TITLE = 'Two shows from one Korean company on the same chart cost each other half a place';
const DESC =
  `Across ${data.pairs} matched pairs in ${data.titleMarketCellsCompared} title-and-market runs, a Korean series `
  + `sits at rank ${data.withSiblingRank} on the weeks another series from the same company is on that country's `
  + `chart, against ${data.aloneRank} on the weeks it is alone — a difference of ${data.difference} of one place.`;

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Dataset',
  name: TITLE,
  description: DESC,
  creator: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'K Culture Wire' },
  isBasedOn: ['https://www.netflix.com/tudum/top10', 'https://www.wikidata.org'],
};
---

<WikiTip title={TITLE} description={DESC} jsonLd={jsonLd}
  canonical="https://www.kculturewire.com/siblings">
  <h1>{TITLE}</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    A country's Netflix chart has ten places on it. A company with two shows out at once is using two
    of them. Whether that is a problem is a scheduling question with money attached, and it is
    answerable from the weekly lists.
  </p>
  <p class="q">{data.question}</p>

  <section>
    <h2>The answer is half a place</h2>
    <p class="note lead-in"><b>How this is counted.</b> {data.unit}</p>
    <div class="scroll-x">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>A Korean series on a country's chart</th><th>Average rank</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="nm">On the weeks it was there alone</td><td class="tot">{data.aloneRank}</td></tr>
          <tr class="edge"><td class="nm">On the weeks a series from the same company was also there</td>
            <td class="tot">{data.withSiblingRank}</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="nm">Difference</td><td class="tot">{data.difference}</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="warn">
      <b>{data.difference} of one place, on a chart with ten of them.</b> It is in the direction a
      scheduler would fear — a title sits lower when its stablemate is beside it — and it is small.
      A company weighing whether to hold a title back for a month is weighing about half a rank.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Why every comparison is inside one title, at one age</h2>
    <p class="note lead-in">{data.whyAgeFixed}</p>
    <div class="scroll-x">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Age of the title</th><th>Pairs</th><th>Alone</th><th>With a sibling</th><th>Difference</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {data.byAge.map((b) => (
            <tr>
              <td class="nm">{b.band}</td>
              <td>{b.pairs}</td>
              <td>{b.aloneRank}</td>
              <td>{b.withSiblingRank}</td>
              <td class="tot">{b.difference}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      All four bands point the same way, and the last column never reaches a whole place. The
      comparison never leaves a single series in a single country, so the title's own strength, its
      company and that country's appetite for Korean shows are all fixed and cannot produce the gap.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What we threw away, and the version of this that was wrong</h2>
    <div class="scroll-x">
      <table>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="nm">Weeks of charts</td><td class="tot">{data.weeksSpanned}</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="nm">Chart rows read</td><td class="tot">{data.rowsRead.toLocaleString('en-US')}</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="nm">Korean series rows with a company attached</td><td class="tot">{data.koreanRowsWithCompany.toLocaleString('en-US')}</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="nm">Of those, rows with a sibling on the same chart</td><td class="tot">{data.weeksWithASibling.toLocaleString('en-US')}</td></tr>
          <tr class="edge"><td class="nm">Weeks discarded — no week of the same age to compare against</td>
            <td class="tot">{data.weeksDroppedNoMatchingAge.toLocaleString('en-US')}</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="nm">Pairs that survived</td><td class="tot">{data.pairs}</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="warn">
      <b>Far more weeks were discarded than kept.</b> A week only counts if the same series, in the
      same country, at a comparable age, also spent a week without a sibling. Most runs never
      overlap a stablemate at all, and those contribute nothing.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      <b>The first version of this measurement was wrong, and it is worth saying how.</b> We began by
      matching weeks at exactly the same age — week 4 against week 4. That left 205 pairs, because a
      title never reaches the same week number twice in one country: the only matches were runs where
      Netflix's week counter had reset. The sample was not small, it was <em>strange</em>. Matching
      inside age bands instead gives {data.pairs} pairs across {data.titleMarketCellsCompared} runs,
      at the cost of allowing up to a week of slack inside a band. That cost is stated here rather
      than hidden.
    </p>
  </section>

  <KcwRelatedArticles page="/siblings" />

  <section class="src">
    <h2>Where these numbers come from, and what they cannot answer</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Netflix and Wikidata</b> — {data.source}</li>
      <li><b>Why this cannot say why.</b> {data.cannotAnswer}</li>
      <li>
        <b>Rank 1 is the top.</b> A larger number is a worse position, so a positive difference means
        the title sat lower when its sibling was there.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Russia is excluded</b> throughout this site, because Netflix withdrew from it and the
        remaining weeks are not comparable with the others.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">
      <a href="/foothold">Does an earlier hit help the next one</a> ·{' '}
      <a href="/who-makes-it">How many companies fill half the shelf</a> ·{' '}
      <a href="/about">How we count</a>
    </p>
  </section>
</WikiTip>
